package cajeta.net.dns;

import cajeta.lang.String;
import cajeta.net.NetException;

/**
 * The hostname **does not resolve** — the authoritative *name does not
 * exist* outcome of a [`Dns.resolve`](Dns.cajeta) lookup (plan item
 * **NET-2.5**). This is the DNS-layer peer of a 404: the query reached a
 * resolver and got back a definitive "no such name" (`NXDOMAIN`) or "the
 * name exists but has no address of the requested family" answer, as
 * opposed to a *transport / server* failure (those become
 * [`ResolutionFailedException`](ResolutionFailedException.cajeta)).
 *
 * ## When it is raised
 *
 * [`NetErrors.fromResolveErrno`](../NetErrors.cajeta) maps these two
 * normalized `cajeta_resolve_err` ordinals (mirrored from
 * [`runtime/native/cajeta_net_getaddrinfo.c`](../../../../native/cajeta_net_getaddrinfo.c))
 * to this subtype:
 *
 *   - `NONAME` (1) — `EAI_NONAME` / `WSAHOST_NOT_FOUND`: no such name.
 *   - `NODATA` (2) — `EAI_NODATA` / `WSANO_DATA`: the name is valid but
 *     has no record of the requested address family (e.g. a V6-only
 *     filter against a name with only A records).
 *
 * Both are **definitive negatives** — retrying the *same* query will
 * give the same answer (unlike a transient `AGAIN`), so they are the
 * `UnknownHost` bucket the NET-2.4 TTL cache may later negatively cache.
 *
 * ## The `kind` / `resolveErrno` split
 *
 * Like the other higher-layer subtypes (see
 * [`docs/stdlib/net/Errors.md`](../../../../../docs/stdlib/net/Errors.md)),
 * this does **not** classify a socket `cajeta_net_err`, so its inherited
 * [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) is the coarse `KIND_OTHER` (99) — its
 * *type* is the primary discriminant. The fine-grained DNS cause rides
 * on [`resolveErrno`](#resolveErrno): the exact `cajeta_resolve_err`
 * ordinal, so a caller that wants to distinguish "no such name" from
 * "no record of that family" can, without a second `instanceof` test.
 *
 * Constructed directly (no `super(...)` chaining — the compiler still
 * rejects it for `Throwable` subtypes; each subtype sets the inherited
 * `message`/`cause`/`kind` and its own field, exactly as
 * [`MalformedAddressException`](../MalformedAddressException.cajeta) sets
 * `position`).
 *
 * Recoverable, not Unrecoverable: callers either catch it (or the
 * [`NetException`](../NetException.cajeta) root) or declare it in their
 * throws clause, mirroring the
 * [`cajeta.io.file`](../../io/file/IoException.cajeta) discipline.
 */
public class UnknownHostException extends NetException {
    /**
     * The normalized `cajeta_resolve_err` ordinal that classified this
     * lookup as a definitive negative — `1` (NONAME, no such name) or
     * `2` (NODATA, name has no record of the requested family). The
     * coarse [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) is `KIND_OTHER` (99); this
     * is the precise DNS discriminant.
     */
    public int32 resolveErrno;

    public UnknownHostException(String message, int32 resolveErrno) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 99;   // NetException.KIND_OTHER — DNS failures are discriminated by type, not errno
        this.resolveErrno = resolveErrno;
    }
}
